# Git Safety — Never Run Git For the User

The user runs **all** git operations themselves. You must **never** execute git commands that write history or touch the remote — not even when the user asks you to commit, push, amend, or open a PR.

## Always Forbidden (Never Run These)

- `git commit` (including `--amend`)
- `git push` / `git push --force`
- `git add` for the purpose of committing on their behalf
- Branch creation, rebases, merges intended for commit/push
- `gh pr create`, `gh pr merge`, or any remote/git write operation

## When the User Asks to Commit or Push

Do **not** run it. Give them **copy-paste terminal commands** instead — for example:

```bash
git add path/to/changed/files
git commit -m "their message here"
git push
```

Include only the files and message that apply. They run it in their own terminal.

Asking for a **commit message**, **PR title**, or **what to run** means provide text or commands only — never execute them.

## No AI Attribution in Commits

Never suggest commit messages or commands that add trailers crediting Cursor, an AI agent, or any bot — for example `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`.

## Default Behavior

After finishing code changes, summarize what changed and stop. Do not offer to commit or push. If git is relevant, offer the commands for them to copy — do not run them.
